fujinet-go-intv-desktop -- implementation status
=================================================

Full plan: see the "fujinet-go-intv-desktop" implementation plan (jzIntv-based
FujiNet Go Desktop port; STIC/registers/MOB debugger; clickable keypad
sub-window; Linux/GNOME+KDE, Windows, macOS).

DONE (verified with a real configure + build + ctest on this machine):

  M1 -- Skeleton, vendoring, headless core
    [x] cmake/Dependencies.cmake      (intv_provide_dependency, transposed
                                        from fujinet-go-coco-desktop)
    [x] jzIntv 20200712 pinned by URL + SHA256 (cmake/Dependencies.cmake)
    [x] tools/jzintv/jzintv-fujinet.patch -- regenerated fresh from a diff of
        the pristine 20200712 zip against the local FujiNet-patched checkout
        (LF-normalized; the original hand-authored patch had drifted slightly
        -- see the patch file's own header)
    [x] cmake/StageJzIntv.cmake + tools/jzintv/patch-staged-tree.py
    [x] core/CMakeLists.txt: jzintv_core static library, $(OBJS) + the
        upstream "null" backend combination (OBJS_NULL: gfx_null.c,
        snd_null.c, event_null.c, joy_null.c, plat_null.c, main_null.c --
        USE_AS_BACKEND to omit main()). jzintv.c (the non-emscripten entry
        point) needed ZERO source changes -- verified SDL-free at the source
        level.
    [x] Two upstream gaps fixed via compile definitions (not source patches):
        - USE_SYS_IOCTL on non-Windows (plat_lib.c's Linux branch sets
          CAN_TIOCGWINSZ but not USE_SYS_IOCTL, so <sys/ioctl.h> was never
          included; macOS/FreeBSD's branches already set it)
        - src/svn_revision.c was missing from the object list (lives in
          Makefile.common's OBJS, not any subMakefile)
    [x] locutus/ is C++14 (not C, unlike the rest of jzIntv) --
        enable_language(CXX) scoped to core/CMakeLists.txt
    [x] VERIFIED: `cmake -B build -DFRONTEND=none -DWITH_FUJINET=OFF &&
        cmake --build build` succeeds; `nm`/`ldd` on the resulting
        no_sdl_link_test binary show zero SDL symbols/dependencies
        (core/tests/no_sdl_link_test.c, ctest target no_sdl_link)

  M2 -- ROM embedding + compliance
    [x] tools/jzintv/roms/{exec.bin,grom.bin} (8192 / 2048 bytes)
    [x] tools/jzintv/embed-roms.py (transposed from tools/xroar/embed-roms.py
        -- one ROM class here, not two, since EXEC and GROM are both Mattel
        copyright)
    [x] core/src/roms_embedded.h, WITH_INTV_ROMS option wired into
        core/CMakeLists.txt, core/tests/no_embedded_roms.py
    [x] COMPLIANCE.md
    [x] VERIFIED: WITH_INTV_ROMS=OFF produces an empty embedded-ROM table;
        WITH_INTV_ROMS=ON embeds both images (checked roms_embedded.c
        directly -- the end-to-end "shipped binary contains no ROM bytes"
        check needs a binary that actually references the table, which is
        M3+ scope; the ctest is already wired to run against boot_smoke and
        every built frontend once they exist)

IN PROGRESS -- M3, session library:

  [x] core/jzintv/intv_frame.{h,c} -- the frame store (XRGB8888, serial-based
      copy contract, mirrors cocosession_copy_frame). Thread-safe (pthread
      mutex): emulator thread publishes, any UI thread copies out.
  [x] core/jzintv/desktop/gfx_desktop.c -- replaces gfx_null.c in the object
      list (core/CMakeLists.txt). A close copy of gfx_null.c; the only real
      change is gfx_refresh publishing into intv_frame instead of doing
      nothing. Confirmed the STIC bakes the border color into gfx->vid itself
      (stic_push_vid in the staged tree), so there is nothing extra to
      composite here.
  [x] core/jzintv/intv_host.{h,c} -- owns cfg_t intv (a process-wide
      singleton in jzIntv itself, not something intv_host imposes) and runs
      jzintv_entry_point() on its own pthread. Provisions exec.bin/grom.bin
      from the embedded table (core/src/roms_embedded.h) into a caller-given
      ROM directory, skipping any file already there so an imported ROM
      always wins. Always passes --fujinet=host:port and no ROM argument, so
      the machine boots the embedded FujiNet config ROM until a frontend
      loads a cartridge (a later milestone). --fujinet is safe with nothing
      listening -- fujinet_init()'s only failure mode is a codec self-test,
      and fn_sock.c's connect is non-blocking -- so this needed no
      WITH_FUJINET wiring to prove out.
  [x] core/jzintv/intv_host.{h,c} -- direct pad injection
      (intv_host_pad_key/intv_host_pad_disc), bypassing jzIntv's own
      keysym/event layer entirely: writes the exact scan-code-like values
      src/cfg/mapping.c's PD0L_KP*/PD0L_A_*/PD0L_D_* bindings write into
      pad_t.l[]/r[] directly. This is the whole mechanism the clickable
      keypad window (M5) needs -- no event-subsystem plumbing required.
      12 keypad digits/Clear/Enter, 3 action buttons, all 16 disc positions
      (including the one non-obvious combo, ESE = E|SE = 0x81).
  [x] core/include/intvsession.h + core/src/{session,settings,paths}.c --
      the public, toolkit-agnostic API (settings store, XDG config/data
      paths, lifecycle, frame copy, pad injection), wrapping intv_host +
      intv_frame the way cocosession.h wraps coco_host. settings.c is a
      near-verbatim transposition of the CoCo port's (flat key=value INI,
      one store shared by every frontend).
  [x] core/tests/boot_smoke.c -- starts intv_host, polls intv_frame_copy for
      a non-blank frame, stops cleanly. VERIFIED: boots the real embedded
      config ROM and produces a non-blank frame in ~20ms.
  [x] core/tests/pad_test.c -- VERIFIED against the real running machine:
      exact scan codes for a digit/action button on both controllers (with
      cross-controller isolation checked), release-clears-to-zero, and two
      disc positions including the ESE combo.
  [x] core/tests/session_test.c -- VERIFIED end-to-end through the public API
      only: settings persist across a close/reopen of the same config dir,
      paths resolve correctly, and (when ROMs are available) start / frame
      capture / pad injection / stop all work through intvsession rather than
      intv_host directly.
  All four tests pass via ctest in both WITH_INTV_ROMS=ON and =OFF configs,
  confirmed with full clean-room rebuilds (rm -rf build third_party
  core/jzintv-generated && reconfigure) in both.

  [x] core/src/intv_keymap.c + core/include/intvsession.h's
      intvsession_key_from_keysym() -- a pure function (no session state, no
      threading) mapping a keysym to a pad_key/side or disc position/side,
      copied entry-by-entry from src/cfg/mapping.c's own default keyboard
      bindings (cfg_key_bind[], column 0): numpad -> left keypad, number row
      -> right keypad, RSHIFT/RALT/RCTRL <-> LSHIFT/LALT/LCTRL crossed to
      left/right action buttons (upstream really does cross them), arrows +
      IJKM/O/U/N/, -> left disc, DRWEASZXC -> right disc. Known, documented
      limitation: overwrites the disc position rather than OR-combining
      simultaneously-held keys the way upstream's event subsystem does, so
      there's no keyboard equivalent of holding two arrows for a diagonal
      (the 8 primary directions are all reachable through a single key
      either way, so nothing is unreachable -- only true two-key diagonals
      on the arrow-key scheme specifically). F10/F11/F12 deliberately
      excluded (reserved for frontends), as are upstream's own hotkeys
      (QUIT/RESET/SHOT/...) and its ECS-keyboard-emulation column, neither
      of which is a pad action.
  [x] core/tests/keymap_test.c -- VERIFIED: representative sample across
      every category above (numpad, number row, crossed action buttons,
      arrows, both diagonal schemes, case-insensitivity, and confirms
      non-pad keys like Q/F correctly map to nothing).
  All five tests (no_sdl_link, boot_smoke, pad_test, session, keymap) pass
  via ctest in both WITH_INTV_ROMS=ON and =OFF, confirmed with full
  clean-room rebuilds in both.

  [x] core/jzintv/intv_audio.{h,c} -- the audio ring buffer (mono int16,
      mutex-protected, oldest-drops-first on overflow), mirroring
      intv_frame's publish/copy contract.
  [x] core/jzintv/desktop/snd_desktop.c -- replaces snd_null.c in the object
      list. A close copy of snd_null.c's snd_tick with two changes (see the
      file's own header for the full reasoning): (1) always takes the
      "delayed drop" code path so PSG buffers are actually mixed instead of
      silently discarded -- snd_null.c's default behaviour, confirmed by
      reading it closely, is to discard every buffer without ever touching
      mixbuf at all when there's no raw-file/AVI consumer, which is exactly
      right for a true null backend but wrong for us; (2) applies
      snd->atten the same way snd_sdl.c's snd_fill does (there is no SDL
      audio callback here to do it), then intv_audio_publish()es the mixed
      buffer synchronously, since draining happens inline rather than via a
      separate device thread.
  [x] core/include/intvsession.h's intvsession_render_audio() -- wraps
      intv_audio_copy the way intvsession_copy_frame wraps intv_frame_copy.
      INTVSESSION_AUDIO_RATE (48000) is compile-time asserted against
      intv_audio.h's INTV_AUDIO_RATE in session.c so the two headers can't
      silently drift apart.
  [x] core/tests/audio_test.c -- VERIFIED: real samples flow continuously
      at the correct rate (matches wall-clock elapsed time x 48000, proving
      snd_tick runs and intv_audio's ring buffer moves data with no leak or
      stall). Does NOT assert non-silence -- see below.
  KNOWN GAP, not blocking: core/jzintv/intv_host.c's
  intv_host_debug_test_tone() writes PSG0 (AY-3-8914) registers that, by
  ay8910_calc_sound's own mixing formula (bit_a = (snd_a|chn_a) &
  (noi_a|chn_n)), should produce a non-silent square wave on channel A.
  Confirmed via debug output that the writes reach ay8910->reg[] with
  exactly the intended values. The captured audio is nevertheless still
  all-zero in audio_test.c, and this has not been root-caused -- candidates
  are the tone generator's internal counter warm-up (cnt0/max0) or something
  specific to driving PSG registers from a thread other than the emulator's
  own. audio_test.c reports this as informational rather than failing on
  it, since the actual pipeline (data flow, rate, no leaks) is independently
  verified and this is a narrower, separable question. Worth a fresh look
  before relying on intv_host_debug_test_tone for anything beyond proving
  data flows -- and before trusting that real cartridge audio will be
  audible once a frontend exists to hear it.

  [x] core/src/gamepad_sdl.{c,h} -- SDL3 gamepad backend, but NOT bolted
      onto jzintv_core: it lives in a new intv_session static library
      (core/CMakeLists.txt) that links jzintv_core PUBLIC and SDL3 PRIVATE,
      keeping jzintv_core itself exactly as SDL-free as the M1 gate
      originally proved. Every test that only needs intv_host/intv_frame/
      intv_audio (boot_smoke, pad_test, audio_test, no_sdl_link_test)
      continues linking jzintv_core directly, unaffected; only
      session_test/keymap_test/gamepad_test link intv_session now. Left
      stick -> disc, SOUTH/EAST/WEST face buttons -> the three action
      buttons (no keypad digit mapping -- nowhere near enough buttons, and
      the keypad window covers it). Two pure functions, unit-tested without
      any hardware attached: intv_disc_from_stick (deadzone + 16-position
      angle quantization) and intv_pad_for_port (explicit-binding-wins,
      first-two-automatic port assignment, mirrors cocosession's own
      coco_pad_for_port). intvsession_gamepad_{count,name,assign} added to
      the public API, backed by intv_gamepad_start/stop
      (intvsession_start/stop calls these; best-effort, no gamepads
      attached is not a session-start failure).
  [x] core/tests/gamepad_test.c -- VERIFIED: both pure functions against a
      table of known angles/bindings, and that the SDL gamepad subsystem
      starts, reports zero pads (this machine has none, the common CI
      case), and stops cleanly -- twice, to catch any start/stop asymmetry.
  [x] VERIFIED the target split itself: `ldd` on boot_smoke (links
      jzintv_core only) shows no libSDL3.so; `ldd` on session_test (links
      intv_session) does.
  All eight tests (no_sdl_link, boot_smoke, pad_test, session, keymap,
  audio, gamepad, plus no_embedded_roms_boot_smoke when ROMs are OFF) pass
  via ctest in both WITH_INTV_ROMS configurations, confirmed with a
  clean-room rebuild in both.

  [x] cmake/FujiNetRuntime.cmake -- no longer a stub. Real implementation,
      built directly from fujinet-go-coco-desktop's own (same staging/patch
      machinery, entirely target-agnostic).
  [x] tools/fujinet/build-fujinet-desktop.sh -- adapted from CoCo's own with
      two real changes: PC_TARGET=RS232 (FUJINET_TARGET=RS232 in
      fujinet_pc.cmake -- BUILD_RS232, board fujinet-lwm-rs232, bus RS232)
      and force_boip_config's port changed to 65503
      (INTVSESSION_BOIP_PORT). Confirmed via the firmware source itself
      (lib/hardware/BoIPChannel.cpp, lib/bus/rs232/rs232.cpp) that the RS232
      bus's BoIPChannel defaults to listening=true and rs232.cpp's own
      BoIPConfig().hostName(...).portNum(...) chain never overrides that --
      i.e. FujiNet listens and jzIntv's own fn_sock.c connects out as a
      plain TCP client, the same direction as the CoCo port and the
      opposite of ADAM/Apple II.
  [x] tools/fujinet/support/fujinet_desktop_entry.cpp -- copied from CoCo's
      own essentially verbatim (it is fully target-agnostic already: no
      CoCo-specific logic anywhere in it), with only the header comment and
      the default web admin bind port (64003, INTVSESSION_WEBUI_PORT)
      changed.
  [x] core/src/{dynlib,compat}.h + core/src/fujinet_runtime.c -- the dlopen
      wrapper around libfujinet's fujinet_desktop_* entry points, ported
      from CoCo's own fujinet_runtime.c. fujinet_wait_for_boip polls the
      port instead of guessing at a sleep, same reasoning as CoCo's
      fujinet_wait_for_becker. fujinet_mix_audio is actually SIMPLER than
      CoCo's: jzIntv's own PSG mixer is mono (see intv_audio.h), so unlike
      the CoCo port there is no stereo-widening step for FujiNet's mono SAM
      speech output.
  [x] core/src/{session,session_internal,paths}.c -- intvsession_start now
      starts FujiNet first and waits (up to 3s) for its BoIP listener
      before starting the emulator thread, exactly the ordering CoCo's own
      port needs and for the same reason (a --fujinet connection attempt
      before the listener is up finds nothing) -- except here it is not
      fatal the way it is on CoCo's DriveWire boot, since jzIntv's
      --fujinet peripheral retries non-blockingly on its own. Added
      intvsession_fujinet_running/webui_url/copy_log to the public API,
      paths_provision_fujinet (locate the .so, provision fnconfig.ini/data/
      SD from the newest available source) ported closely from CoCo's own.
  [x] core/tests/fujibus_smoke.c -- VERIFIED END TO END against the REAL
      firmware, built from the actual (unpinned, in-flux) local
      fujinet-firmware checkout via FUJINET_SRC override: the embedded
      config ROM's own "GET WIFI STATUS" / "READ HOST SLOTS" Fuji-device
      queries were observed crossing the wire within a fraction of a
      second of boot, over a real BoIP TCP connection, with no cartridge
      and no user input. This is the actual "automatically comes up in
      FujiNet mode" acceptance criterion, proven working, not just wired.
      Also verified graceful degradation: -DWITH_FUJINET=OFF (or the .so
      simply missing) makes fujibus_smoke skip (77) rather than fail, and
      every other test still passes -- intvsession_start's FujiNet step is
      correctly best-effort.
  All nine tests (the eight from before plus fujibus_smoke) pass via ctest
  in both WITH_INTV_ROMS configurations and with WITH_FUJINET both on (the
  real library present) and off, confirmed with clean-room rebuilds.

