  Still open in M3: the silent-tone gap noted in the audio section above.
  dasm_test and stic_test are M4 scope (the debugger engine), not M3's.

M3 IS COMPLETE (see above for the full FujiNet runtime item that closed it
out).

IN PROGRESS -- M4, debugger engine (CPU side done; STIC/MOB view and
symbols still open):

  [x] core/include/intvdebug.h + core/debugger/debugger.c -- built on
      jzIntv's own hook (cp1600_instr_tick), not a stepped path of our own:
      cp1600_t already calls a registered callback between instructions
      whenever steps_remaining reaches 0, and jzIntv's own console debugger
      (src/debug/debug.c) already registers itself there unconditionally at
      cfg_init() time -- intvdebug_set_engaged(d,1) simply re-registers our
      own hook in its place (a single function pointer, not a chain; safe,
      since the console debugger is never reachable here with no stdin to
      read commands from). Breakpoints use jzIntv's own native mechanism
      (cp1600_set_breakpt/cp1600_clr_breakpt, which substitutes the decoded
      opcode at that address rather than checking a table every
      instruction) including its existing one-shot flag (CP1600_BKPT_ONCE,
      self-clearing on hit) for step_over/step_out's temporary breakpoints.
      Threading matches cocodebug.h's own model exactly: the emulator
      thread blocks (pthread_cond_wait) right inside the hook while paused
      -- there is no other place progress could be stopped, and no
      separate "paused" flag the run loop checks elsewhere.
  [x] FOUND AND FIXED A REAL HANG while building this: cp1600_run() reaches
      its do_instr_tick label -- and so calls the registered hook -- once
      per outer timeslice regardless of step_count (whenever the inner
      instruction loop simply runs out of cycles to spend, not only when
      steps_remaining hits 0), so a naive hook that always blocks
      unconditionally freezes the machine the instant the debugger is
      engaged, before any pause is ever requested. Fixed by blocking only
      when there is an actual reason to (a real/one-shot breakpoint just
      fired, or single-step mode is active) -- verified by debug_test.c
      hanging before the fix and passing cleanly after.
  [x] Also fixed a macro-precedence bug while writing this: cp1600.h's
      CP1600_PK/CP1600_WR substitute their first argument textually into
      `c->periph.bus`, so passing the expression &intv.cp1600 directly (not
      a plain identifier) parsed as &(intv.cp1600->periph.bus) --  wrong
      precedence, a real compile error. Fixed by assigning a local
      `cp1600_t *const cp = &intv.cp1600;` first, matching how every other
      call site of these macros in the staged tree already does it.
  [x] step_over: detects a JSR-family instruction (JSR/JSRE/JSRD/JSRi all
      share that mnemonic prefix, nothing else in the CP-1610 set does) via
      intvdebug_disassemble, then sets a one-shot breakpoint just past it
      and runs free rather than single-stepping through the whole
      subroutine.
  [x] step_out: honestly approximate, and documented as such in
      intvdebug.h -- the CP-1610 has no hardware call stack, so which
      register holds the return address is not recoverable once already
      inside a routine. Breaks on whatever R5 currently holds (the
      convention EXEC and most game software actually use, confirmed
      empirically in debug_test.c: r5 held a plausible EXEC return address
      the whole time). A routine entered through a different register
      falls back to running free rather than stopping somewhere wrong.
  [x] core/tests/debug_test.c -- VERIFIED against the real running
      machine: engage/disengage, pause (waits for the actual instruction
      boundary), register read (PC correctly mirrors r[7], and lands in
      one of the machine's own mapped ROM ranges -- by the time a pause
      actually lands a few ms into boot, execution is well past EXEC init
      and running the embedded config ROM's own code, not stuck in EXEC,
      which is what an earlier, wrong test assumption had to be corrected
      for), disassembly (produces real text at the right address with a
      plausible instruction length), memory read (EXEC ROM is real
      content, not zero-filled) and write (a scratchpad-RAM poke round-
      trips), breakpoint toggle/list, single-step (advances the stop
      serial and re-pauses), resume (leaves the paused state), and a clean
      disengage. All nine tests continue to pass via ctest, clean-room
      rebuild confirmed.

  [x] core/include/intvstic.h + core/debugger/sticview.c -- the STIC
      register/mode/MOB/GRAM-GROM-card/palette view. Every offset and bit
      layout was read directly out of the staged tree's src/stic/stic.c by
      finding where each field is actually consumed (stic_do_mob,
      stic_draw_mobs, stic_ctrl_wr_internal, the raw[0x28-0x32] reads), not
      from a datasheet -- see the header's own comment.
  [x] intvstic_render_backtab -- closes the BACKTAB (background card grid)
      rendering gap noted above. Both STIC modes implemented straight from
      stic_draw_cstk/stic_draw_fgbg's own formulas (line-numbers checked
      against the staged tree, not recalled): Color Stack mode's ordinary
      cards (gr_idx = (card&0xFF8) & (GRAM-select ? gram_mask : ~0), fg_clr
      = ((card>>9)&0x8)|(card&7), cs_idx/bg advance on card bit 13) and its
      "colored squares" special case (card&0x1800==0x1000, four 2x2-pixel
      quadrants, color 7 in any quadrant meaning "current color-stack top" --
      and bit 11 being forced to 0 by the detection mask itself means that
      case can never actually trigger for the fourth quadrant, which the
      code mirrors rather than "fixes"); Foreground/Background mode's
      gr_idx = card&0x9F8, fg_clr = card&7, bg_clr =
      ((card>>9)&0xB)|((card>>11)&0x4).
  [x] core/tests/stic_test.c -- VERIFIED against the real running machine:
      writes known values directly into intv.stic.raw/gmem, then confirms
      the snapshot/decode reports exactly those values back out -- mode,
      GRAM size, full color stack, border color, every MOB field (position,
      both size dimensions, both flips, color, GRAM/priority/visible flags,
      collision bits), an untouched MOB correctly decoding to invisible,
      and a hand-written diagonal bit pattern round-tripping through the
      card-sheet renderer pixel-for-pixel.
  [x] BACKTAB rendering coverage added to stic_test.c -- since
      intvstic_render_backtab is a pure function of the snapshot, these run
      against locally-built snapshots (only intv.gfx.palette, already
      populated by the boot above, is read outside the snapshot):
      Foreground/Background mode's fg/bg pixel colors from a single card;
      Color Stack mode's two ordinary cards proving the stack-advance bit
      actually moves bg from stack slot 0 to slot 1; and the "colored
      squares" special case's four quadrants, including Pix0=7 correctly
      resolving to the live color-stack top rather than a literal color 7.
      All eleven ctest cases pass, clean-room rebuild confirmed in both
      WITH_INTV_ROMS ON and OFF configurations.
  [x] CAUGHT A REAL BUG while writing the card-sheet test: assumed GROM was
      512 cards (matching some other 8-bit VDP-ish chips' convention);
      stic_test.c's first run failed on the card-rendering assertion, which
      led to checking stic_init's own GROM-copy loop
      (`for(i=0;i<2048;i++) stic->gmem[i]=grom_img[i];`) -- GROM is a fixed
      2048 BYTES, i.e. 256 cards at 8 bytes/card, not 512. Fixed in
      intvstic_card_count, the GRAM-offset comment in sticview.c, and the
      test's own offsets; all corrected instances cross-checked against
      that same init loop rather than guessed twice.

  [x] core/debugger/symbols.{c,h} -- name<->address table, modelled
      directly on the CoCo port's own cocosymtab. The built-in table is
      deliberately minimal (just $1000 EXEC_ENTRY and $9C00
      FUJINET_MAILBOX) rather than the usual small library of documented
      ROM entry points CoCo/ADAM/MSX ship: this project has no verified
      primary source in hand for documented Intellivision EXEC subroutine
      addresses (no ROM disassembly, no vendor manual, nothing locally
      checkable), and CoCo's own symtab.c states the principle this
      followed -- "a wrong address in a debugger is worse than a missing
      one" -- so nothing was transcribed from memory with no way to verify
      it. Both built-in entries ARE independently confirmed, from the
      staged jzIntv tree's own source (cp1600_reset's r[7]=0x1000, and
      FUJINET_WINDOW_SIZE's placement in fujinet.h) cross-checked against
      intv_host's own boot log. intvsymtab_load speaks as1600's real -s
      output format, confirmed against jzIntv's own loader for the same
      files (src/debug/debug.c) rather than assumed -- undefined symbols
      ("????????") and out-of-range addresses are skipped exactly as that
      loader does.
  [x] core/tests/symtab_test.c -- VERIFIED (pure function, no emulator
      boot needed): both built-ins, add/lookup in both directions, a real
      as1600-format file (including its undefined-symbol and out-of-range
      skip rules) loading correctly, newest-wins tie-breaking on a shared
      address, and clear_user leaving the built-ins alone.

  M4 IS NOW FULLY COMPLETE, including the BACKTAB rendering gap closed
  above. cp1600dasm.c as a standalone wrapper is still not written (not
  required yet -- intvdebug_disassemble already covers every use so far).

NOT STARTED:

  M5 -- GNOME frontend (GTK4 + libadwaita): main window, debugger window,
        keypad sub-window (both hand controllers, clickable keypad/disc/
        action buttons -- no precedent in any sibling project, build this
        one first and port its layout to the other three).

  M6 -- KDE (Qt6 Widgets), macOS (AppKit), Windows (Win32) frontends.

  M7 -- Packaging: icons, flatpak manifests, NSIS installer, CPack DEB/RPM/
        TGZ, CI workflows (linux/windows/macos/flatpak/release), all release
        builds with -DWITH_INTV_ROMS=OFF.
        Icon: same icon as the other FujiNet Go Desktop clients, but on a
        dark green (#1e4912) background (per user note, 2026-08-10).
